// The search for what a function's own tests already say
// (docs/native/optimizer-design.md §5.1), and for the test worth making at its
// entry. Nothing here is trusted or proved: what it finds is a plan the
// replayer checks (`function_ir_replay.hthr`) by the rules of
// `function_ir_rules.hthr`, and a guard `function_ir_guard.hthr` checks.
//
// One walk of a function keeps, for each word, the range its node and its
// operands' ranges give it, and the rule's step that derives that range
// (`Seen::pass`). A comparison whose operands' ranges lie apart is decided:
// the walk writes the derivations the rule reads, then the rule, and a
// conditional on the bit is its arm (`Plan::Arm`). An arm starts from what
// its bit says of the comparison's operands (`Seen::taking`). A loop's body
// starts from the ranges of the inputs its passes keep (`Seen::carried`).
// Where a load or a store indexes by a comparison's left operand, the
// decided bit is written after the comparison as a constant, which is what
// the lowering reads the bound from.
//
// A word narrowed and widened again while its range is within the narrow
// width is a move of it (`Seen::replaced`): the widening is dropped and the
// move written, for the pass that reads through moves to take up. A load's
// word is a byte, which the replayer's facts say of it unasked, so the two
// resizes after a byte load go; an or's range is its operands' added, so
// the two after four bytes joined go as well.
//
// A parameter that words are compared below is a length, and the least
// value that would decide every such comparison met is the bound to test at
// the function's entry (`Function::guard`). The compiler asks that of a
// function once it is inlined and tidied, which is where its tests are, and
// puts the test on the function as written (`Function::guard_over`,
// `Function::find_guarded` in `function_ir_optimize.hthr`).
//
// A range bounds a word by constants, which decides nothing of an index a
// caller computed. For those the walk keeps orders between words
// (`Ordered`, after Bodik, Gupta and Sarkar's ABCD, "ABCD: eliminating
// array bounds checks on demand", PLDI 2000: an index is in bounds where a
// path of orders leads from it to the length, and here the path is the
// derivation the replayer checks). A bit taken gives an order between its
// comparison's operands (`Seen::ordered_by`); a word that is another and a
// constant added is ordered with it where an order shows the sum did not
// wrap; and a comparison is decided where the orders known join into the
// one it asks (`Seen::ordered`).
//
// Where the words compared below a length are all one word and constants
// added (a span: the bytes of a word read at `base`, `base + 1`, …), the
// test worth making at the entry is of the base itself: that `base` and
// the greatest constant added do not wrap, and stay below the length
// (`span_tests`). The words the base is computed from are computed again
// for the test, at new ids (`cone_renamed`); the pass that reads through
// copies then finds the body's own computation of them a repeat
// (`Step::Again`), so what the test says of its sum is said of the body's.
//
// Bytes loaded from a span and joined by shifts and ors are a word, and
// one load reads a word (docs/native/optimizer-design.md §4.1). The walk keeps,
// for a word, that it is so many bytes of a buffer from an index, shifted
// (`Join`): a byte load starts one, a shift moves it, and an or of two
// that are side by side in the buffer and in the word is the longer one,
// where the orders known say the second starts where the first ends
// (`Seen::meeting`). However the bytes were joined, the same fact is
// reached. Four bytes unshifted are a load of four bytes swapped: where
// the code has none, the walk asks for one (`Asked`,
// `Function::find_worded` puts it), and where it has, the or that
// completes the join is written as a move of that load's word, after
// which nothing reads the byte loads. What the walk keeps of the buffers
// it forgets where the replayer's facts do.
//
// A comparison's bit stays written as a constant after it where an access
// reads its bound from it: a load or a store indexed by the comparison's
// left, or one of several bytes whose last byte that is. A constant an
// earlier walk wrote for a bit this one decides again goes
// (`Seen::replaced`), so a test nothing reads any more is left for the
// sweep.
//
// A range also says what a sum is. Where the operand's range has room for
// the constant a sum adds, the sum is the two as numbers
// (`Seen::sum_orders`, rules 34 and 35): so the test that it wrapped is
// decided (`Seen::ordered`), and bytes at a constant offset are side by
// side as bytes at a word are. A difference that cannot borrow has a
// range, and an or of decided bits is decided (`Seen::or_decided`), so the
// test a function tested at its entry carries is decided where the
// function is inlined into a loop whose bound gives the index its range.
import function_ir;
import function_ir_operation;
import function_ir_eval;
import function_ir_state;
import function_ir_writes;
import function_ir_views;
import function_ir_stage;
import function_ir_fresh;
import function_ir_guard;
import function_ir_match;
import function_ir_replay;
import function_ir_rule;
import function_ir_rules;
import bitvec;
import bool;
import list;
import logic;
import nat;
import nat_map;
import option;
import pair;

// How a word's range reaches the replayer's facts: the rule's step, and
// the words whose ranges the rule reads.
record How { needs: List<Nat>, step: Step }

// A word that is a word and a constant added: the word, and the
// constant's id and value.
record Sum { of: Nat, constant: Nat, value: Nat }

// An order the replayer's facts can be given, and the steps that give it:
// none once they are written.
record Known { order: Order, why: List<Step> }

// What is known of words' order, for the comparisons no range decides.
record Ordered {
    sums: NatMap<Sum>,
    known: List<Known>,
    // The nodes that compute a word from the function's parameters alone,
    // each after what it reads: such a word can be computed at the
    // function's entry. A parameter's are none.
    cones: NatMap<List<Node>>,
}

fn Ordered::none() → Ordered { Ordered(NatMap::empty(), [], NatMap::empty()) }

// A test wanted at the function's entry: the word `base`, which `cone`
// computes from the parameters, and `least` are at most the parameter
// `length`. `uses` comparisons wait on it.
record Span { base: Nat, cone: List<Node>, least: Nat, length: Nat, uses: Nat }

// A word that is bytes of a buffer joined (`Fact::Joined`): `count` bytes
// of `buffer` from the index `at`, which is the word `base` and the
// constant `past` added, shifted left by `shift`. `why` are the steps that
// give the fact: none once they are written.
record Join { buffer: Nat, base: Nat, past: Nat, at: Nat, count: Nat, shift: Nat, why: List<Step> }

// A load of four bytes the code has, swapped and widened into `word`: the
// bytes of `buffer` from the index `at`, the first the most significant.
record Word { buffer: Nat, at: Nat, loaded: Nat, swapped: Nat, word: Nat }

// A load of four bytes the code does not have and a join would be: of
// `buffer` at the index `at`, wanted after the byte load that writes
// `after`, which is where every test the bytes passed is behind.
record Asked { buffer: Nat, at: Nat, after: Nat }

// A node a rule would read the word of and no word is computed by, as
// `a + b` where `(x + a) + b` is to be `x + (a + b)`: written to `unput()`
// (function_ir_match), and wanted once the words `waits` are written,
// which are the words the node the rule fits reads.
record Helper { node: Node, waits: List<Nat> }

// What is known of the buffers as they are where the walk stands, which is
// what the replayer's facts keep of them (`Facts::forget`): the joins by
// the word each is, the four-byte loads by the word each stage writes, and
// the last byte load passed. And `ends`, which is of the whole function:
// for the index of a load or a store of several bytes, how far past it the
// last byte is.
record Joins { of: NatMap<Join>, loads: NatMap<Word>, swaps: NatMap<Word>, words: List<Word>, last: Option<Nat>, ends: NatMap<Nat> }

fn Joins::none(ends: NatMap<Nat>) → Joins { Joins(NatMap::empty(), NatMap::empty(), NatMap::empty(), [], None, ends) }

// What a function's undecided comparisons wait on. For a parameter, the
// least value that would have every comparison met so far of a word with
// it be one: a parameter words are compared below is a length, and a test
// that it is long enough is the one worth making. And the spans below a
// length that would decide the comparisons of words no range is known of.
// `words` are the loads its joins of bytes wait on.
// `nodes` are the nodes a rule's other side reads that no word computes yet.
record Wants { least: NatMap<Nat>, spans: List<Span>, words: List<Asked>, nodes: List<Helper> }

record Seen {
    // The comparison that writes a bit, and the constant that writes a word.
    made: NatMap<Node>,
    // What is known of a word, and how.
    range: NatMap<Range>,
    how: NatMap<How>,
    // The words whose range the steps written so far derive.
    proved: NatMap<Bool>,
    // The bits known constant.
    bits: NatMap<Bool>,
    // What the arms entered owe, written with the first decision after.
    pending: List<Step>,
    // The words a load or a store indexes by.
    indexes: NatMap<Bool>,
    // The parameter of the function a word is, through moves and what a
    // loop does not change.
    origin: NatMap<Nat>,
    // Kept through arms and loops.
    wants: Wants,
    order: Ordered,
    joins: Joins,
    // The rules the search applies by matching (function_ir_rules).
    tables: Tables,
}

// A parameter is computed by nothing.
fn Ordered::parameter(self order: Ordered, id: Nat) → Ordered { Ordered(order.sums, order.known, nat_map_set(order.cones, id, [])) }

fn cone_writes(cone: List<Node>, id: Nat) → Bool {
    match cone { Nil => False, Cons(node, rest) => node_output(node).id.same(id) || cone_writes(rest, id) }
}

// Two cones as one: the first, then the second's nodes it does not have.
fn cone_join(first, second: List<Node>) → List<Node> {
    match second {
        Nil => first,
        Cons(node, rest) => match cone_writes(first, node_output(node).id) { True => cone_join(first, rest), False => cone_join(first ++ [node], rest) },
    }
}

// The cones of `ids` as one, where each has one.
fn Ordered::cones_of(self order: Ordered, ids: List<Nat>, so_far: List<Node>) → Option<List<Node>> {
    match ids {
        Nil => Some(so_far),
        Cons(id, rest) => match nat_map_get(order.cones, id) { None => None, Some(cone) => order.cones_of(rest, cone_join(so_far, cone)) },
    }
}

// No cone longer than this is worth computing at a function's entry.
fn cone_most() → Nat { 6 }

// What a node adds: a sum of a word with a constant `made` has, and the
// cone of the word it writes where what it reads have cones.
fn Ordered::noting(self order: Ordered, made: NatMap<Node>, node: Node) → Ordered {
    Ordered(
        match node {
            Add(w, o, l, r) => match w.same(64) {
                False => order.sums,
                True => match nat_map_get(made, r) {
                    None => order.sums,
                    Some(writer) => match writer {
                        Const(width, _, v) => match width.same(64) { True => nat_map_set(order.sums, o, Sum(l, r, v)), False => order.sums },
                        _ => order.sums,
                    },
                },
            },
            _ => order.sums,
        },
        order.known,
        match node.pure() {
            False => order.cones,
            True => match order.cones_of(node.reads(), []) {
                None => order.cones,
                Some(before) => match before.len().leb(cone_most()) {
                    True => nat_map_set(order.cones, node_output(node).id, before ++ [node]),
                    False => order.cones,
                },
            },
        })
}

fn Seen::start(indexes: NatMap<Bool>, ends: NatMap<Nat>, tables: Tables) → Seen {
    Seen(NatMap::empty(), NatMap::empty(), NatMap::empty(), NatMap::empty(), NatMap::empty(), [], indexes, NatMap::empty(), Wants(NatMap::empty(), [], [], []),
        Ordered::none(), Joins::none(ends), tables)
}

fn Seen::wanting(self seen: Seen, wants: Wants) → Seen {
    Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, seen.bits, seen.pending, seen.indexes, seen.origin, wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)
}

// `id` is the word `source` is.
fn Seen::from(self seen: Seen, id, source: Nat) → Seen {
    match nat_map_get(seen.origin, source) {
        None => seen,
        Some(parameter) => Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, seen.bits, seen.pending, seen.indexes,
            nat_map_set(seen.origin, id, parameter), seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables),
    }
}

fn Seen::of_parameters(self seen: Seen, parameters: List<Nat>) → Seen {
    match parameters {
        Nil => seen,
        Cons(parameter, rest) => Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, seen.bits, seen.pending, seen.indexes,
            nat_map_set(seen.origin, parameter, parameter), seen.wants, seen.order.parameter(parameter), seen.joins, seen.tables).of_parameters(rest),
    }
}

// No bound past this is worth a test at a function's entry.
fn want_most() → Nat { 4294967296 }

// A word was compared below the word `id`: if that is a parameter, the
// comparison is one where it is at least `least`.
fn Seen::want(self seen: Seen, id, least: Nat) → Seen {
    match nat_map_get(seen.origin, id) {
        None => seen,
        Some(parameter) => match least.leb(want_most()) {
            False => seen,
            True => match nat_map_get(seen.wants.least, parameter) {
                None => seen.wanting(Wants(nat_map_set(seen.wants.least, parameter, least), seen.wants.spans, seen.wants.words, seen.wants.nodes)),
                Some(already) => match least.leb(already) {
                    True => seen,
                    False => seen.wanting(Wants(nat_map_set(seen.wants.least, parameter, least), seen.wants.spans, seen.wants.words, seen.wants.nodes)),
                },
            },
        },
    }
}

fn Seen::of(self seen: Seen, id: Nat) → Range { match nat_map_get(seen.range, id) { None => Range::any(), Some(range) => range } }

fn flagged(flags: NatMap<Bool>, id: Nat) → Bool { match nat_map_get(flags, id) { None => False, Some(flag) => flag } }

// A word's range, which `step` derives from the ranges of `needs`.
fn Seen::learn(self seen: Seen, id: Nat, range: Range, needs: List<Nat>, step: Step) → Seen {
    Seen(seen.made, nat_map_set(seen.range, id, range), nat_map_set(seen.how, id, How(needs, step)), seen.proved, seen.bits, seen.pending,
        seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)
}

// A word's range the replayer's facts hold with no step: a load's byte.
fn Seen::known(self seen: Seen, id: Nat, range: Range) → Seen {
    Seen(seen.made, nat_map_set(seen.range, id, range), seen.how, nat_map_set(seen.proved, id, True), seen.bits, seen.pending,
        seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)
}

// The node that writes `id`, for a later node's rule to read.
fn Seen::making(self seen: Seen, id: Nat, node: Node) → Seen {
    Seen(nat_map_set(seen.made, id, node), seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, seen.bits, seen.pending, seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)
}

fn Seen::ordering(self seen: Seen, order: Ordered) → Seen {
    Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, seen.bits, seen.pending, seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, order, seen.joins, seen.tables)
}

// A node that computes a word is kept as what made it, whatever its kind:
// a rule's statement may read any node, a program's rule included. A node
// that reads or writes a span is not, since the replayer's facts have it
// only where it stands.
fn Seen::computing(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Seen {
    match node.pure() { True => seen.making(node_output(node).id, node), False => seen }
}

// A node passed, for a later node's rule and for what is known of order.
fn Seen::noted(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Seen {
    shared(seen.computing(node), λ kept => kept.ordering(kept.order.noting(kept.made, node)))
}

record Explained { steps: List<Step>, proved: NatMap<Bool> }

// The steps that derive the ranges of `ids` not yet derived, what each
// reads first.
fn explain(fuel: Nat, how: NatMap<How>, proved: NatMap<Bool>, ids: List<Nat>) → Explained {
    match fuel {
        Zero => Explained([], proved),
        Succ(less) => match ids {
            Nil => Explained([], proved),
            Cons(id, rest) => match flagged(proved, id) {
                True => explain(less, how, proved, rest),
                False => match nat_map_get(how, id) {
                    None => explain(less, how, proved, rest),
                    Some(found) => shared(explain(less, how, proved, found.needs), λ first =>
                        shared(explain(less, how, nat_map_set(first.proved, id, True), rest), λ later =>
                            Explained(first.steps ++ Cons(found.step, later.steps), later.proved))),
                },
            },
        },
    }
}

// What follows a node's own step, and what is known after it.
record Passed { after: List<Step>, seen: Seen }

// A bit decided: what is owed, the derivations the rule reads, the rule,
// and what to write into the code.
fn Seen::decided(self seen: Seen, c: Nat, bit: Bool, needs: List<Nat>, step: Step, emit: List<Step>) → Passed {
    shared(explain(100, seen.how, seen.proved, needs), λ why =>
        Passed(seen.pending ++ (why.steps ++ Cons(step, emit)),
            Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, why.proved, nat_map_set(seen.bits, c, bit), [], seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)))
}

// Steps owed with the next decision.
fn Seen::owing(self seen: Seen, steps: List<Step>) → Seen {
    Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, seen.bits, seen.pending ++ steps, seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)
}

fn Order::same(self a: Order, b: Order) → Bool { a.low.same(b.low) && (a.add.same(b.add) && (a.high.same(b.high) && a.more.same(b.more))) }

// An order known that gives `wanted`.
fn known_find(known: List<Known>, wanted: Order) → Option<Known> {
    match known { Nil => None, Cons(first, rest) => match first.order.gives(wanted) { True => Some(first), False => known_find(rest, wanted) } }
}

// An order from `base` to `length` with room for `value` and one more:
// what says the word `value` past `base` is below `length`.
fn known_room(known: List<Known>, base, length, value: Nat) → Option<Known> {
    match known {
        Nil => None,
        Cons(first, rest) => match first.order.low.same(base) && (first.order.high.same(length) && (value + first.order.more + 1).leb(first.order.add)) {
            True => Some(first),
            False => known_room(rest, base, length, value),
        },
    }
}

// An order's steps are written: it is known with none.
fn known_written(known: List<Known>, order: Order) → List<Known> {
    known.map(λ (k: Known) => match k.order.same(order) { True => Known(k.order, []), False => k })
}

fn Seen::written(self seen: Seen, order: Order) → Seen {
    seen.ordering(Ordered(seen.order.sums, known_written(seen.order.known, order), seen.order.cones))
}

// Two orders that meet at a word, as one with what both add taken from
// both sides (`order_join`, rule 24).
fn Known::then(self first: Known, second: Known) → Known {
    shared(first.order.add + second.order.add, λ up => shared(first.order.more + second.order.more, λ down =>
        shared(match up.leb(down) { True => up, False => down }, λ both =>
            Known(Order(first.order.low, up - both, second.order.high, down - both),
                first.why ++ (second.why ++ [Derive(24, [first.order.low, first.order.high, second.order.high, first.order.add, first.order.more, second.order.add, second.order.more, up - both, down - both])])))))
}

// What `new` gives with each order known that ends where it starts or
// starts where it ends.
fn known_joined(known: List<Known>, new: Known) → List<Known> {
    match known {
        Nil => [],
        Cons(first, rest) => shared(known_joined(rest, new), λ later =>
            shared(match first.order.high.same(new.order.low) && !first.order.low.same(new.order.high) { True => Cons(first.then(new), later), False => later }, λ after =>
                match new.order.high.same(first.order.low) && !new.order.low.same(first.order.high) { True => Cons(new.then(first), after), False => after })),
    }
}

// No more orders than this are joined with a new one.
fn known_most() → Nat { 24 }

fn Seen::knowing(self seen: Seen, new: Known) → Seen {
    seen.ordering(Ordered(seen.order.sums, match seen.order.known.len().leb(known_most()) {
        True => Cons(new, known_joined(seen.order.known, new) ++ seen.order.known),
        False => Cons(new, seen.order.known),
    }, seen.order.cones))
}

// What a comparison's bit says of its operands' order in the arm taken
// where it is `bit` (rules 20 and 21). Where it is clear and the left is
// the right and a constant added, the sum did not wrap, so it is the right
// and the constant, both ways (rules 25 and 26).
fn Seen::ordered_by(self seen: Seen, c, l, r: Nat, bit: Bool) → Seen {
    match bit {
        True => seen.knowing(Known(Order(l, 1, r, 0), [Derive(20, [l, r, c])])),
        False => shared(seen.knowing(Known(Order(r, 0, l, 0), [Derive(21, [l, r, c])])), λ known => match nat_map_get(known.order.sums, l) {
            None => known,
            Some(sum) => match sum.of.same(r) {
                False => known,
                True => known.knowing(Known(Order(r, sum.value, l, 0), [Derive(21, [l, r, c]), Derive(25, [r, sum.value, l, sum.constant])]))
                    .knowing(Known(Order(l, 0, r, sum.value), [Derive(21, [l, r, c]), Derive(26, [r, sum.value, l, sum.constant])])),
            },
        }),
    }
}

// A decided bound written after its comparison, where a load or a store
// indexes by the comparison's left: what the lowering reads.
// So is the comparison of the last byte's index, for a load or a store of
// several bytes: the index and how far past it that byte is.
fn Seen::ends_at(self seen: Seen, l: Nat) → Bool {
    match nat_map_get(seen.order.sums, l) {
        None => False,
        Some(sum) => match nat_map_get(seen.joins.ends, sum.of) { None => False, Some(last) => last.same(sum.value) },
    }
}

fn Seen::bound_written(self seen: Seen, c, l: Nat) → List<Step> {
    match flagged(seen.indexes, l) || seen.ends_at(l) { True => [Emit(Const(1, c, 1))], False => [] }
}

// An order from `base` with room for `value`: what says `base` and
// `value` added is no more than a word, so the sum is that.
fn known_roomy(known: List<Known>, base, value: Nat) → Option<Known> {
    match known {
        Nil => None,
        Cons(first, rest) => match first.order.low.same(base) && (value + first.order.more).leb(first.order.add) {
            True => Some(first),
            False => known_roomy(rest, base, value),
        },
    }
}

record Stepped { steps: List<Step>, seen: Seen }

fn Seen::proving(self seen: Seen, proved: NatMap<Bool>) → Seen {
    Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, proved, seen.bits, seen.pending, seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)
}

// The steps after which the facts have that the word `at`, a word and a
// constant added, is the two as numbers, both ways: by an order from the
// word with room for the constant (rules 27 and 28), or by the word's
// range with room for it below a word's top (rules 34 and 35).
fn Seen::sum_orders(self seen: Seen, at: Nat) → Option<Stepped> {
    match nat_map_get(seen.order.sums, at) {
        None => None,
        Some(sum) => match known_roomy(seen.order.known, sum.of, sum.value) {
            Some(k) => Some(Stepped(k.why ++ [
                Derive(27, [sum.of, k.order.high, sum.value, k.order.add, k.order.more, at, sum.constant]),
                Derive(28, [sum.of, k.order.high, sum.value, k.order.add, k.order.more, at, sum.constant]),
            ], seen.written(k.order))),
            None => shared(seen.of(sum.of), λ range => match (range.high + sum.value).leb(word_top()) {
                False => None,
                True => shared(explain(100, seen.how, seen.proved, [sum.of]), λ why => Some(Stepped(why.steps ++ [
                    Derive(34, [sum.of, sum.value, range.low, range.high, at, sum.constant]),
                    Derive(35, [sum.of, sum.value, range.low, range.high, at, sum.constant]),
                ], seen.proving(why.proved)))),
            }),
        },
    }
}

// The same owed, with the two orders known.
fn Seen::summed(self seen: Seen, at: Nat) → Option<Seen> {
    match nat_map_get(seen.order.sums, at) {
        None => None,
        Some(sum) => match seen.sum_orders(at) {
            None => None,
            Some(stepped) => Some(stepped.seen.owing(stepped.steps).knowing(Known(Order(sum.of, sum.value, at, 0), []))
                .knowing(Known(Order(at, 0, sum.of, sum.value), []))),
        },
    }
}

// A comparison an order decides: one known says its left is below its
// right (rule 22), or its right at most its left (rule 23); or its left is
// a word and a constant added, and an order from that word to its right
// has room for the constant and one more (rules 28 and 24, then 22).
fn Seen::ordered_known(self seen: Seen, c, l, r: Nat) → Option<Passed> {
    shared(seen.bound_written(c, l), λ emit =>
        match known_find(seen.order.known, Order(l, 1, r, 0)) {
            Some(k) => Some(seen.written(k.order).owing(k.why).decided(c, True, [], Derive(22, [l, r, c]), emit)),
            None => match known_find(seen.order.known, Order(r, 0, l, 0)) {
                Some(k) => Some(seen.written(k.order).owing(k.why).decided(c, False, [], Derive(23, [l, r, c]), [])),
                None => match nat_map_get(seen.order.sums, l) {
                    None => None,
                    Some(sum) => match known_room(seen.order.known, sum.of, r, sum.value) {
                        None => None,
                        Some(k) => Some(seen.written(k.order).owing(k.why ++ [
                            Derive(28, [sum.of, r, sum.value, k.order.add, k.order.more, l, sum.constant]),
                            Derive(24, [l, sum.of, r, 0, sum.value, k.order.add, k.order.more, 1, 0]),
                        ]).decided(c, True, [], Derive(22, [l, r, c]), emit)),
                    },
                },
            },
        })
}

// The same, and where no order known decides it: with an operand that is
// a sum known not to wrap as the two it adds. So a sum compared with its
// own operand, the test that it did not wrap, is decided where the
// operand's range has room.
fn Seen::ordered(self seen: Seen, c, l, r: Nat) → Option<Passed> {
    match seen.ordered_known(c, l, r) {
        Some(passed) => Some(passed),
        None => match seen.summed(l).and_then(λ wider => wider.ordered_known(c, l, r)) {
            Some(passed) => Some(passed),
            None => seen.summed(r).and_then(λ wider => wider.ordered_known(c, l, r)),
        },
    }
}

fn spans_add(spans: List<Span>, new: Span) → List<Span> {
    match spans {
        Nil => [new],
        Cons(first, rest) => match first.base.same(new.base) && first.length.same(new.length) {
            True => Cons(Span(first.base, first.cone, match new.least.leb(first.least) { True => first.least, False => new.least }, first.length,
                first.uses + new.uses), rest),
            False => Cons(first, spans_add(rest, new)),
        },
    }
}

fn Seen::span(self seen: Seen, base, least, length: Nat) → Seen {
    match nat_map_get(seen.order.cones, base) {
        None => seen,
        Some(cone) => match least.leb(want_most()) {
            False => seen,
            True => seen.wanting(Wants(seen.wants.least, spans_add(seen.wants.spans, Span(base, cone, least, length, 1)), seen.wants.words, seen.wants.nodes)),
        },
    }
}

// A word no range is known of was compared below the word `r`: if that
// is a parameter and the word, or the word it is a constant past, can be
// computed at the function's entry, the comparison is one where that word
// and the constant and one more are at most the parameter.
fn Seen::spanning(self seen: Seen, l, r: Nat) → Seen {
    match nat_map_get(seen.origin, r) {
        None => seen,
        Some(parameter) => match parameter.same(r) {
            False => seen,
            True => match nat_map_get(seen.order.sums, l) { Some(sum) => seen.span(sum.of, sum.value + 1, r), None => seen.span(l, 1, r) },
        },
    }
}

// What the walk has, as a rule's statement is read against it
// (function_ir_match).
fn Seen::walked(self seen: Seen) → Walked { Walked(seen.made, seen.range, seen.bits) }

// A bit a rule decides at the node that writes it: the rule's step after
// what it reads is derived, and `emit` where the bit is set.
fn Seen::settled_bit(self seen: Seen, node: Node, emit: List<Step>) → Option<Passed> {
    decided_among(seen.tables.rewriting_at(node), node, seen.walked()).and_then(λ found => found.bit().and_then(λ bit =>
        Some(seen.decided(node_output(node).id, bit, found.needs, Derive(found.place, found.numbers), match bit { True => emit, False => [] }))))
}

// A comparison: decided where a rule decides it, as where its operands'
// ranges lie apart (rules 7 and 8), or an order known does. Where a load
// or a store indexes by its left, the bit is written after it, which is
// what the lowering reads (docs/native/optimizer-design.md §5.1).
fn Seen::compared(self seen: Seen, c, l, r: Nat, node: Node) → Passed {
    shared(Seen(nat_map_set(seen.made, c, node), seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, seen.bits, seen.pending, seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables), λ noted =>
        match noted.settled_bit(node, seen.bound_written(c, l)) {
            Some(passed) => passed,
            None => match noted.ordered(c, l, r) {
                Some(passed) => passed,
                None => shared(seen.of(l), λ a => Passed([], match (a.high + 1).leb(want_most()) { True => noted.want(r, a.high + 1), False => noted.spanning(l, r) })),
            },
        })
}

// An or of bits: decided where a rule decides it, as where both are clear
// (rule 36) or either is set (rules 37 and 38), as the guard of several
// bounds is where each bound is.
fn Seen::or_decided(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Passed {
    match seen.settled_bit(node, []) { Some(passed) => passed, None => Passed([], seen) }
}

// The range of the word a node writes, where a rule that gives ranges has
// one from the node and the ranges of what it reads.
fn Seen::bounding(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Seen {
    match ranged_among(seen.tables.ranging_at(node), node, seen.walked(), None) {
        None => seen,
        Some(found) => seen.learn(node_output(node).id, found.range, found.needs, Derive(found.place, found.numbers)),
    }
}

// What the walk keeps of a node passed beyond its range.
fn Seen::tracked(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Passed {
    match node {
        Move(w, o, s) => Passed([], match w.same(64) { True => seen.from(o, s), False => seen }),
        Resize(from, to, o, _) => Passed([], match to.same(64) && from.leb(63) {
            True => match from.same(32) { True => seen.making(o, node), False => seen },
            False => match from.same(64) { True => seen.making(o, node), False => seen },
        }),
        BufferLoad(o, _, _) => Passed([], seen.known(o, Range(0, 255))),
        BufferStore(o, _, _, _) => Passed([], seen.known(o, Range(0, 255))),
        UnsignedLessThan(w, c, l, r) => match w.same(64) { True => seen.compared(c, l, r, node), False => Passed([], seen) },
        BitOr(w, o, _, _) => match w.same(1) { True => seen.making(o, node).or_decided(node), False => Passed([], seen) },
        ByteSwap(w, o, _) => match w.same(32) { True => Passed([], seen.making(o, node)), False => Passed([], seen) },
        _ => Passed([], seen),
    }
}

// A node passed: its range by the rules, then the rest.
fn Seen::pass(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Passed { seen.bounding(node).tracked(node) }

// A rule's instance at a node that writes another node for it: one a
// rule's statement gives, else one a registered learner proposes.
fn Seen::instance(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Option<Matched> {
    match matched_among(seen.tables.rewriting_at(node), node, seen.walked()) {
        Some(found) => Some(found),
        None => proposed_among(seen.tables.learners, seen.tables.placed, node, seen.walked()),
    }
}

// A bit's move, which the lowering has no code for (`Node::lower`): a rule
// that would write one for a node (`x | 0 = x` over bits) is not applied.
fn Node::moves_bit(self node: Node) → Bool { match node { Move(w, _, _) => w.same(1), _ => False } }

fn Seen::has_settled(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Bool { match seen.settled_bit(node, []) { Some(_) => True, None => False } }

fn made_constant(made: NatMap<Node>, id: Nat) → Bool { match nat_map_get(made, id) { Some(by) => match by { Const(_, _, _) => True, _ => False }, None => False } }

// Whether a rule decides the bit a node writes. The walk then keeps the
// node and the bit: a rule that writes another node for it (`x | 0 = x`
// where both are clear) would leave a move the walk knows less of, and the
// tests that read the bit undecided.
//
// And an index that is a sum of two constants stays that sum. The joins of
// byte loads into a word read an index as a base and a constant
// (`Ordered.sums`), as the replayer's rules about their order do, so with
// `60 + 1` written `61` four loads side by side would stay four. So does
// the sum of an index several bytes are read at and a constant: the
// comparison of the last byte's index is read as that sum (`ends_at`),
// and the lowering refuses the access without it.
fn Seen::settles(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Bool {
    match node {
        UnsignedLessThan(w, _, _, _) => w.same(64) && seen.has_settled(node),
        BitOr(w, _, _, _) => w.same(1) && seen.has_settled(node),
        Add(_, o, l, r) => match made_constant(seen.made, l) && made_constant(seen.made, r) {
            False => False,
            True => match nat_map_get(seen.indexes, o) { Some(_) => True, None => match nat_map_get(seen.joins.ends, l) { Some(_) => True, None => False } },
        },
        _ => False,
    }
}

// Whether the walk looks for what a rule reads among the nodes rules wrote
// others for, and weighs a spelling by what it frees: a walk after nodes
// were put (`Function::find_helped`, function_ir_optimize), which is told
// by `asking`. Every other walk is spared the work.
fn Seen::lends(self seen: Seen) → Bool { match seen.tables.asking { Zero => False, Succ(_) => True } }

// The node that wrote a word before a rule wrote another for it, kept
// beside the new one (`Walked::earlier`, function_ir_match), with a mark
// at `unput()` that there is one.
fn Seen::respelled(self seen: Seen, old: Node) → Seen {
    match seen.lends() && (old.pure() && !old.kind().same(17)) { True => seen.making(unput(), old).making(old.alike_key(), old), False => seen }
}

// The steps that write the node an instance gives for the node it is of.
fn Seen::rewritten(self seen: Seen, found: Matched) → Option<Passed> {
    match found.needs {
        Nil => shared(seen.pass(found.node), λ written =>
            Some(Passed([Drop, Derive(found.place, found.numbers), Emit(found.node)] ++ written.after, written.seen.computing(found.node)))),
        Cons(_, _) => shared(explain(100, seen.how, seen.proved, found.needs), λ why =>
            shared(Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, why.proved, seen.bits, [], seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)
                .pass(found.node), λ written =>
                Some(Passed(Cons(Drop, seen.pending ++ (why.steps ++ ([Derive(found.place, found.numbers), Emit(found.node)] ++ written.after))),
                    written.seen.computing(found.node))))),
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A search through a word's other spellings
//
// The walk above takes the first rule whose statement fits a node as it is
// written, so `(a + b) - b = a` does not fit `(b + a) - b`, and a rule
// that only pays after another has fired is not found. Here every node the
// rules give for the word, and for each word it reads, is followed until
// nothing new appears or the budget ends, and the cheapest is written with
// the steps that derive it (docs/native/optimizer-rules-design.md §9.4). A
// rule's node is over words the function has, so a word is a class and its
// spellings the class's members; no word is made.

// Another node that computes a word, and the steps that derive that it does
// from what the replayer has at the node.
record Spelling { node: Node, why: List<Step> }

fn spellings_have(found: List<Spelling>, node: Node) → Bool {
    match found { Nil => False, Cons(first, rest) => first.node.same(node) || spellings_have(rest, node) }
}

// The instances at a spelling as spellings, those `known` has left out.
fn spellings_new(found: List<Matched>, from: Spelling, known: List<Spelling>) → List<Spelling> {
    match found {
        Nil => [],
        Cons(first, rest) => match spellings_have(known, first.node) {
            True => spellings_new(rest, from, known),
            False => shared(Spelling(first.node, from.why ++ [Derive(first.place, first.numbers)]), λ made =>
                Cons(made, spellings_new(rest, from, Cons(made, known)))),
        },
    }
}

// The spellings the rules reach from `todo`, `fuel` of them followed.
fn spellings_closed(fuel: Nat, tables: Tables, had: Walked, lend: Bool, todo: List<Spelling>, done: List<Spelling>) → List<Spelling> {
    match fuel {
        Zero => done ++ todo,
        Succ(less) => match todo {
            Nil => done,
            Cons(first, rest) => shared(spellings_new(instances_among(tables.rewriting_at(first.node), first.node, had, lend), first, done ++ todo), λ more =>
                spellings_closed(less, tables, had, lend, rest ++ more, done ++ [first])),
        },
    }
}

// What a node costs the code, as far as choosing among spellings goes: a
// move is read through and swept, a constant is one instruction with no
// operand to keep, and the rest are alike.
fn Node::weight(self node: Node) → Nat { match node { Move(_, _, _) => 0, Const(_, _, _) => 1, _ => 2 } }

fn depth_larger(a, b: Nat) → Nat { match a.leb(b) { True => b, False => a } }

// How far back the deepest of the words `ids` is computed from, `fuel`
// nodes at most: a parameter and a constant from nowhere.
fn made_deepest(fuel: Nat, made: NatMap<Node>, ids: List<Nat>) → Nat {
    match fuel {
        Zero => 1,
        Succ(less) => match ids {
            Nil => 0,
            Cons(id, rest) => depth_larger(match nat_map_get(made, id) {
                None => 0,
                Some(node) => match node { Const(_, _, _) => 0, _ => made_deepest(less, made, node.reads()) + 1 },
            }, made_deepest(less, made, rest)),
        },
    }
}

fn made_depths(made: NatMap<Node>, ids: List<Nat>) → Nat {
    match ids { Nil => 0, Cons(id, rest) => made_deepest(6, made, [id]) + made_depths(made, rest) }
}

fn ids_have(ids: List<Nat>, id: Nat) → Bool { match ids { Nil => False, Cons(first, rest) => first.same(id) || ids_have(rest, id) } }

// Whether writing `other` for `node` leaves unread each word `node` read
// and `other` does not: a constant aside, the word is read once in the
// function, by `node`.
fn frees_all(reads: NatMap<Nat>, made: NatMap<Node>, ids: List<Nat>, kept: List<Nat>) → Bool {
    match ids {
        Nil => True,
        Cons(id, rest) => (ids_have(kept, id) || match nat_map_get(made, id) {
            Some(by) => match by { Const(_, _, _) => True, _ => match nat_map_get(reads, id) { None => True, Some(n) => n.leb(1) } },
            None => True,
        }) && frees_all(reads, made, rest, kept),
    }
}

fn Seen::frees(self seen: Seen, node: Node, other: Node) → Bool { frees_all(seen.tables.reads, seen.made, node.reads(), other.reads()) }

fn ids_within(ids, among: List<Nat>) → Bool { match ids { Nil => True, Cons(id, rest) => ids_have(among, id) && ids_within(rest, among) } }

// What a spelling of `node` costs: its weight first. Between two of a
// weight, one that reads other words than `node` is cheaper where its
// operands are computed from less far back and it frees what `node` read:
// `x + (a + b)` with `a + b` a constant is so cheaper than `(x + a) + b`
// where nothing else reads `x + a`, which the sweep then takes; where
// something does, the code would keep both sums and a copy. A spelling
// that reads the words `node` does, as one with its operands turned, costs
// what `node` does, and how far back is not looked at for it: that look is
// most of what a spelling would cost to weigh, and nearly every spelling
// is of this kind.
fn Seen::cost(self seen: Seen, node: Node, other: Node) → Nat {
    other.weight() * 64 + match !seen.lends() || ids_within(other.reads(), node.reads()) && ids_within(node.reads(), other.reads()) {
        True => 62,
        False => match (other.weight() + 1).leb(node.weight()) {
            True => 0,
            False => match seen.frees(node, other) {
                False => 63,
                True => shared(made_depths(seen.made, other.reads()), λ depth => match (depth + 1).leb(made_depths(seen.made, node.reads())) && depth.leb(61) {
                    True => depth,
                    False => 63,
                }),
            },
        },
    }
}

// The cheapest of the spellings of `node` cheaper than `most` that the
// lowering has code for, the first of them where several are.
fn Seen::lightest(self seen: Seen, node: Node, found: List<Spelling>, most: Nat, best: Option<Spelling>) → Option<Spelling> {
    match found {
        Nil => best,
        Cons(first, rest) => shared(seen.cost(node, first.node), λ cost => match (cost + 1).leb(most) && !first.node.moves_bit() {
            True => seen.lightest(node, rest, cost, Some(first)),
            False => seen.lightest(node, rest, most, best),
        }),
    }
}

// The spellings of a node where the word `w` it reads is spelled each
// other way the rules give: the steps that derive that spelling come first.
fn Seen::spellings_through(self seen: Seen, node: Node, w: Nat, inner: List<Spelling>) → List<Spelling> {
    match inner {
        Nil => [],
        Cons(first, rest) => spellings_closed(seen.tables.spellings, seen.tables,
            Walked(nat_map_set(seen.made, w, first.node), seen.range, seen.bits), seen.lends(), [Spelling(node, first.why)], [])
            ++ seen.spellings_through(node, w, rest),
    }
}

fn Seen::spellings_reading(self seen: Seen, node: Node, reads: List<Nat>) → List<Spelling> {
    match reads {
        Nil => [],
        Cons(w, rest) => match nat_map_get(seen.made, w) {
            None => seen.spellings_reading(node, rest),
            Some(inner) => match seen.tables.reads_through(node, inner) {
                False => seen.spellings_reading(node, rest),
                True => match spellings_closed(seen.tables.spellings, seen.tables, seen.walked(), seen.lends(), [Spelling(inner, [])], []) {
                    Nil => seen.spellings_reading(node, rest),
                    Cons(_, others) => seen.spellings_through(node, w, others) ++ seen.spellings_reading(node, rest),
                },
            },
        },
    }
}

// Every spelling of the word a node writes: from the node as the walk has
// its operands, then with each operand spelled another way, where a rule
// read at the node reads a node of the operand's kind
// (`Tables::reads_through`): most nodes have no such rule, and following
// their operands' spellings was most of what a searching walk did.
fn Seen::spellings(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → List<Spelling> {
    spellings_closed(seen.tables.spellings, seen.tables, seen.walked(), seen.lends(), [Spelling(node, [])], [])
        ++ seen.spellings_reading(node, node.reads())
}

// A node at which the walk searches: the lightest spelling where one is
// lighter than what the first rule to fit gives, else that rule's node as
// in the plain walk (a rule that reads a range, a learner's proposal).
fn Seen::searched(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Option<Passed> {
    match seen.settles(node) {
        True => None,
        False => shared(seen.instance(node), λ direct =>
            match seen.lightest(node, seen.spellings(node), match direct { Some(found) => seen.cost(node, found.node), None => seen.cost(node, node) }, None) {
                Some(best) => shared(seen.pass(best.node), λ written =>
                    Some(Passed(Cons(Drop, best.why ++ Cons(Emit(best.node), written.after)), written.seen.computing(best.node).respelled(node)))),
                None => direct.and_then(λ found => match found.node.moves_bit() {
                    True => None,
                    False => seen.rewritten(found).and_then(λ passed => Some(Passed(passed.after, passed.seen.respelled(node)))),
                }),
            }),
    }
}

// Whether a node not yet put would be worth its place: the rules give its
// word a cheaper spelling, as a sum of two constants is a constant.
fn Seen::folds(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Bool {
    match seen.lightest(node, spellings_closed(seen.tables.asking, seen.tables, seen.walked(), seen.lends(), [Spelling(node, [])], []), seen.cost(node, node), None) {
        Some(_) => True,
        None => False,
    }
}

fn helpers_have(asked: List<Helper>, node: Node) → Bool {
    match asked { Nil => False, Cons(first, rest) => first.node.alike(node) || helpers_have(rest, node) }
}

fn ids_made(made: NatMap<Node>, ids: List<Nat>) → List<Nat> {
    match ids {
        Nil => [],
        Cons(id, rest) => match nat_map_get(made, id) { Some(_) => Cons(id, ids_made(made, rest)), None => ids_made(made, rest) },
    }
}

// The nodes the rules that fit `node` but for one node would read, where
// no word is computed by it, it folds, and what `node` reads and it does
// not is read by nothing else (the rule's node reads no more of `node`'s
// words than the node put does, so the rule's node then frees them).
fn helpers_asked(table: List<Matchable>, node: Node, seen: Seen, asked: List<Helper>) → List<Helper> {
    match table {
        Nil => asked,
        Cons(m, rest) => helpers_asked(rest, node, seen, match m.helped(node, seen.walked()) {
            None => asked,
            Some(help) => match help.node.pure() && (seen.frees(node, help.node) && (!helpers_have(asked, help.node) && seen.folds(help.node))) {
                False => asked,
                True => match seen.walked().earlier(help.node) {
                    Some(_) => asked,
                    None => Cons(Helper(help.node, ids_made(seen.made, node.reads())), asked),
                },
            },
        }),
    }
}

// The walk at a node it keeps, where it asks: what it would have put.
fn Seen::helping(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Seen {
    match seen.tables.asking {
        Zero => seen,
        Succ(_) => seen.wanting(Wants(seen.wants.least, seen.wants.spans, seen.wants.words, helpers_asked(seen.tables.lending, node, seen, seen.wants.nodes))),
    }
}

// A node a rule's statement fits (function_ir_match), written as the node
// the rule's conclusion gives: the old one is dropped once the facts have
// its equation, the rule gives the new one, and that is written. Where the
// rule reads ranges, what is owed and the steps that derive them come
// first. A word narrowed and widened again within the narrow width is so a
// move of the word it was (rule 15), and a word swapped, widened, narrowed
// and swapped again a move of the word (rule 44).
fn Seen::matched(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Option<Passed> {
    match seen.tables.spellings {
        Zero => seen.instance(node).and_then(λ found => match seen.settles(node) || found.node.moves_bit() {
            True => None,
            False => seen.rewritten(found),
        }),
        Succ(_) => seen.searched(node),
    }
}

// A node the walk writes another for, with the steps that do.
fn Seen::replaced(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Option<Passed> {
    match node {
        // A bit's constant from an earlier walk, where this one decided the
        // bit again: it is written after its comparison where an access
        // still reads it, so the old one goes.
        Const(w, c, v) => match w.same(1) {
            False => None,
            True => match nat_map_get(seen.bits, c) {
                None => None,
                Some(bit) => match v.same(match bit { True => 1, False => 0 }) { True => Some(Passed([Drop], seen)), False => None },
            },
        },
        _ => seen.matched(node),
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Bytes joined into words (docs/native/optimizer-design.md §4.1)

fn Seen::joins_as(self seen: Seen, joins: Joins) → Seen {
    Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, seen.bits, seen.pending, seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, joins, seen.tables)
}

// The buffers may have changed, or the replayer's facts keep nothing of
// them past here: nor does the walk.
fn Seen::forgetting(self seen: Seen) → Seen { seen.joins_as(Joins::none(seen.joins.ends)) }

fn Seen::join_of(self seen: Seen, id: Nat) → Option<Join> { nat_map_get(seen.joins.of, id) }

fn Seen::joined(self seen: Seen, id: Nat, join: Join) → Seen {
    seen.joins_as(Joins(nat_map_set(seen.joins.of, id, join), seen.joins.loads, seen.joins.swaps, seen.joins.words, seen.joins.last, seen.joins.ends))
}

// What is owed is written.
fn Seen::settled(self seen: Seen) → Seen {
    Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, seen.bits, [], seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)
}

// Two joins side by side, `hi` first in the buffer and above in the word.
fn Join::beside(self hi: Join, lo: Join) → Bool {
    hi.buffer.same(lo.buffer) && (hi.base.same(lo.base) && (hi.shift.same(8 * lo.count + lo.shift) && (hi.past + hi.count).same(lo.past)))
}

// The steps after which the facts have that `lo` starts where `hi` ends,
// both ways: each index is the base and its constant, and where `hi` does
// not start at the base the two orders meet there (rule 24).
fn Seen::beside(self seen: Seen, hi, lo: Join) → Option<Stepped> {
    seen.sum_orders(lo.at).and_then(λ second => match hi.past {
        Zero => Some(second),
        Succ(_) => second.seen.sum_orders(hi.at).and_then(λ first => Some(Stepped(second.steps ++ (first.steps ++ [
            Derive(24, [hi.at, hi.base, lo.at, 0, hi.past, lo.past, 0, hi.count, 0]),
            Derive(24, [lo.at, hi.base, hi.at, 0, lo.past, hi.past, 0, 0, hi.count]),
        ]), first.seen))),
    })
}

fn words_find(words: List<Word>, buffer, at: Nat) → Option<Word> {
    match words {
        Nil => None,
        Cons(word, rest) => match word.buffer.same(buffer) && word.at.same(at) { True => Some(word), False => words_find(rest, buffer, at) },
    }
}

// A load wanted for a join, where a byte load to put it after was passed.
fn Seen::asking(self seen: Seen, join: Join) → Seen {
    match seen.joins.last {
        None => seen,
        Some(after) => seen.wanting(Wants(seen.wants.least, seen.wants.spans, Cons(Asked(join.buffer, join.at, after), seen.wants.words), seen.wants.nodes)),
    }
}

// What a node is to the joins: whether it goes, the steps after it, and
// what is known then.
record Joining { drop: Bool, after: List<Step>, seen: Seen }

// An or of two joins side by side is the longer join (rule 31, or 32 where
// the or has the lower one first): the steps owed, those that give the two
// joins and their indexes' order, then the rule. Four bytes unshifted are
// a word: where a load of it was passed the or is a move of that load's
// word (rule 33) and goes, and where none was, one is asked for.
fn Seen::meeting(self seen: Seen, rule, o, above, below: Nat, hi, lo: Join) → Option<Joining> {
    seen.beside(hi, lo).map(λ orders =>
        shared(Join(hi.buffer, hi.base, hi.past, hi.at, hi.count + lo.count, lo.shift, []), λ whole =>
            shared(orders.seen.pending ++ (hi.why ++ (lo.why ++ (orders.steps ++
                [Derive(rule, [hi.buffer, hi.at, lo.at, above, below, hi.count, lo.count, lo.shift, o])]))), λ steps =>
                shared(orders.seen.settled().joined(o, whole), λ after => match whole.count.same(4) && whole.shift.same(0) {
                    False => Joining(False, steps, after),
                    True => match words_find(after.joins.words, whole.buffer, whole.at) {
                        Some(word) => Joining(True, steps ++ [Derive(33, [whole.buffer, whole.at, o, word.loaded, word.word, word.swapped]),
                            Emit(Move(64, o, word.word))], after),
                        None => Joining(False, steps, after.asking(whole)),
                    },
                }))))
}

// A byte load starts a join (rule 29), at the index's base and constant
// where the index is a sum; a shift of a join is one (rule 30); an or of
// two may be. A load of four bytes, its swap and its widening are kept for
// the join they are. Nothing is kept past a store.
fn Seen::joining(self seen: Seen, node: Node) → Joining {
    match node {
        BufferLoad(b, f, x) => Joining(False, [], shared(seen.joined(b, match nat_map_get(seen.order.sums, x) {
            None => Join(f, x, 0, x, 1, 0, [Derive(29, [f, x, b])]),
            Some(sum) => Join(f, sum.of, sum.value, x, 1, 0, [Derive(29, [f, x, b])]),
        }), λ kept => kept.joins_as(Joins(kept.joins.of, kept.joins.loads, kept.joins.swaps, kept.joins.words, Some(b), kept.joins.ends)))),
        ShiftLeft(w, s, a, c) => match w.same(64) {
            False => Joining(False, [], seen),
            True => match seen.join_of(a) {
                None => Joining(False, [], seen),
                Some(j) => Joining(False, [], seen.joined(s, Join(j.buffer, j.base, j.past, j.at, j.count, j.shift + c,
                    j.why ++ [Derive(30, [j.buffer, j.at, a, j.count, j.shift, c, s])]))),
            },
        },
        BitOr(w, o, l, r) => match w.same(64) {
            False => Joining(False, [], seen),
            True => match seen.join_of(l) {
                None => Joining(False, [], seen),
                Some(left) => match seen.join_of(r) {
                    None => Joining(False, [], seen),
                    Some(right) => match (match left.beside(right) {
                        True => seen.meeting(31, o, l, r, left, right),
                        False => match right.beside(left) { True => seen.meeting(32, o, r, l, right, left), False => None },
                    }) {
                        None => Joining(False, [], seen),
                        Some(met) => met,
                    },
                },
            },
        },
        WideLoad(bytes, w, f, i) => match bytes.same(4) {
            False => Joining(False, [], seen),
            True => Joining(False, [], seen.joins_as(Joins(seen.joins.of, nat_map_set(seen.joins.loads, w, Word(f, i, w, 0, 0)), seen.joins.swaps,
                seen.joins.words, seen.joins.last, seen.joins.ends))),
        },
        ByteSwap(width, s, w) => match width.same(32) {
            False => Joining(False, [], seen),
            True => match nat_map_get(seen.joins.loads, w) {
                None => Joining(False, [], seen),
                Some(word) => Joining(False, [], seen.joins_as(Joins(seen.joins.of, seen.joins.loads,
                    nat_map_set(seen.joins.swaps, s, Word(word.buffer, word.at, word.loaded, s, 0)), seen.joins.words, seen.joins.last, seen.joins.ends))),
            },
        },
        Resize(from, to, r, s) => match from.same(32) && to.same(64) {
            False => Joining(False, [], seen),
            True => match nat_map_get(seen.joins.swaps, s) {
                None => Joining(False, [], seen),
                Some(word) => Joining(False, [], seen.joins_as(Joins(seen.joins.of, seen.joins.loads, seen.joins.swaps,
                    Cons(Word(word.buffer, word.at, word.loaded, word.swapped, r), seen.joins.words), seen.joins.last, seen.joins.ends))),
            },
        },
        _ => Joining(False, [], match node.stores() { True => seen.forgetting(), False => seen }),
    }
}

record Swept { steps: List<Step>, seen: Seen }

fn learn_nodes(seen: Seen, nodes: List<Node>) → Swept {
    match nodes {
        Nil => Swept([], seen),
        Cons(node, rest) => match seen.replaced(node) {
            Some(here) => shared(learn_nodes(here.seen, rest), λ later => Swept(here.after ++ later.steps, later.seen)),
            None => shared(seen.helping(node).pass(node), λ here => shared(here.seen.noted(node).joining(node), λ joined =>
                shared(learn_nodes(joined.seen, rest), λ later =>
                    Swept(Cons(match joined.drop { True => Drop, False => Keep }, here.after ++ (joined.after ++ later.steps)), later.seen)))),
        },
    }
}

// The ranges and the steps as the rules a test gives narrow them.
record Narrowed { range: NatMap<Range>, steps: List<Step> }

// What the rules say of other words where the bit the node `at` writes
// is known, each read against what the walk had on entering the arm: a
// word's range is narrowed where a rule says more than is known, by the
// rule's step.
fn narrowed_among(table: List<Matchable>, at: Node, had: Walked, so: Narrowed) → Narrowed {
    match table {
        Nil => so,
        Cons(m, rest) => match m.told_at(at, had) {
            None => narrowed_among(rest, at, had, so),
            Some(told) => shared(range_known(so.range, told.id), λ known => match known.inside(told.range) {
                True => narrowed_among(rest, at, had, so),
                False => narrowed_among(rest, at, had,
                    Narrowed(nat_map_set(so.range, told.id, known.meet(told.range)), so.steps ++ [Derive(told.place, told.numbers)])),
            }),
        },
    }
}

// In the arm taken where the bit the comparison `node` writes is known:
// what the rules say of its operands (rules 9 to 12), owed with the
// derivations the steps read.
fn Seen::narrowed(self seen: Seen, node: Node, l, r: Nat) → Seen {
    shared(explain(100, seen.how, seen.proved, [l, r]), λ why => shared(narrowed_among(seen.tables.testing_at(node), node, seen.walked(), Narrowed(seen.range, [])), λ found =>
        Seen(seen.made, found.range, seen.how, nat_map_set(nat_map_set(why.proved, l, True), r, True), seen.bits,
            seen.pending ++ (why.steps ++ found.steps), seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)))
}

// Where the bit `c` is `bit`: what a comparison's bit says of its operands,
// and a clear or's of the bits it is of (to a depth: a test of several
// bounds is a few).
fn Seen::taking_in(self seen: Seen, depth, c: Nat, bit: Bool) → Seen {
    shared(Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, seen.proved, nat_map_set(seen.bits, c, bit), seen.pending, seen.indexes, seen.origin, seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables), λ known =>
        match nat_map_get(seen.made, c) {
            None => known,
            Some(node) => match node {
                UnsignedLessThan(_, _, l, r) => known.narrowed(node, l, r).ordered_by(c, l, r, bit),
                BitOr(_, _, a, b) => match bit {
                    True => known,
                    False => match depth {
                        Zero => known,
                        Succ(less) => known.owing([Derive(13, [a, b, c]), Derive(14, [a, b, c])]).taking_in(less, a, False).taking_in(less, b, False),
                    },
                },
                _ => known,
            },
        })
}

// In the arm taken where the bit `c` is `bit`.
fn Seen::taking(self seen: Seen, c: Nat, bit: Bool) → Seen { seen.taking_in(8, c, bit) }

fn ranged(ranges: NatMap<Range>, id: Nat) → Bool { match nat_map_get(ranges, id) { None => False, Some(_) => True } }

// The inputs of a loop whose range its passes keep: those a parameter that
// is its own next starts from.
fn Seen::kept(self seen: Seen, inputs, parameters, next: List<Nat>) → List<Nat> {
    match inputs {
        Nil => [],
        Cons(input, more) => match parameters {
            Nil => [],
            Cons(parameter, others) => match next {
                Nil => [],
                Cons(after, later) => match parameter.same(after) && ranged(seen.range, input) {
                    True => Cons(input, seen.kept(more, others, later)),
                    False => seen.kept(more, others, later),
                },
            },
        },
    }
}

// Those ranges as ranges of `ids`, which the replayer's facts say: of the
// parameters as it enters the body, of the outputs after the loop.
fn Seen::carried(self seen: Seen, inputs, parameters, next, ids: List<Nat>, into: Seen) → Seen {
    match inputs {
        Nil => into,
        Cons(input, more) => match parameters {
            Nil => into,
            Cons(parameter, others) => match next {
                Nil => into,
                Cons(after, later) => match ids {
                    Nil => into,
                    Cons(id, further) => shared(seen.carried(more, others, later, further, into), λ rest => match parameter.same(after) {
                        False => rest,
                        True => shared(match nat_map_get(seen.origin, input) {
                            None => rest.origin,
                            Some(word) => nat_map_set(rest.origin, id, word),
                        }, λ origin => match nat_map_get(seen.range, input) {
                            None => Seen(rest.made, rest.range, rest.how, rest.proved, rest.bits, rest.pending, rest.indexes, origin, rest.wants, rest.order, rest.joins, rest.tables),
                            Some(range) => Seen(rest.made, nat_map_set(rest.range, id, range), rest.how, nat_map_set(rest.proved, id, True),
                                rest.bits, rest.pending, rest.indexes, origin, rest.wants, rest.order, rest.joins, rest.tables),
                        }),
                    }),
                },
            },
        },
    }
}

record Learned { plan: Plan, seen: Seen }

// A plan that derives what decides a comparison, has a conditional on a
// decided bit be its arm, and writes a decided bound after its comparison.
fn StructuredRegion::learn(self region: StructuredRegion, seen: Seen) → Learned {
    match region {
        Dataflow(body) => shared(learn_nodes(seen, body.nodes), λ s => Learned(Nodes(s.steps), s.seen)),
        Sequence(prefix, continuation) => shared(learn_nodes(seen, prefix.nodes), λ s =>
            shared(continuation.learn(s.seen), λ c => Learned(Then(s.steps, c.plan), c.seen))),
        Compose(prefix, continuation) => shared(prefix.learn(seen), λ a =>
            shared(continuation.learn(a.seen), λ c => Learned(Both(a.plan, c.plan), c.seen))),
        Conditional(_, condition, _, _, when_true, when_false) => match nat_map_get(seen.bits, condition) {
            // The arm is the code from here on, so what the walk saw in it
            // is seen after it, as the replayer's facts are.
            Some(bit) => shared(match bit { True => when_true.learn(seen), False => when_false.learn(seen) }, λ arm =>
                Learned(Arm(bit, arm.plan), arm.seen)),
            None => shared(when_true.learn(seen.taking(condition, True)), λ yes =>
                shared(when_false.learn(seen.taking(condition, False).wanting(yes.seen.wants)), λ no =>
                    Learned(Arms(yes.plan, no.plan), seen.wanting(no.seen.wants).forgetting()))),
        },
        Call(_, _, _, _) => Learned(Same, seen.forgetting()),
        Loop(inputs, _, parameters, _, body, _, next, outputs, _) => shared(seen.kept(inputs, parameters, next), λ kept =>
            shared(explain(100, seen.how, seen.proved, kept), λ why =>
                shared(body.learn(seen.carried(inputs, parameters, next, parameters, Seen::start(seen.indexes, seen.joins.ends, seen.tables).wanting(seen.wants))), λ inner =>
                    match seen.pending ++ why.steps {
                        Nil => Learned(Body(inner.plan), seen.carried(inputs, parameters, next, outputs, seen.wanting(inner.seen.wants)).forgetting()),
                        Cons(step, steps) => Learned(Derived(Cons(step, steps), inner.plan), seen.carried(inputs, parameters, next, outputs,
                            Seen(seen.made, seen.range, seen.how, why.proved, seen.bits, [], seen.indexes, seen.origin, inner.seen.wants, seen.order, seen.joins, seen.tables)).forgetting()),
                    }))),
        Lend(_, _, _, _) => Learned(Same, seen.forgetting()),
    }
}

fn nodes_indexes(nodes: List<Node>, found: NatMap<Bool>) → NatMap<Bool> {
    match nodes {
        Nil => found,
        Cons(node, rest) => nodes_indexes(rest, match node {
            BufferLoad(_, _, index) => nat_map_set(found, index, True),
            BufferStore(_, _, index, _) => nat_map_set(found, index, True),
            WideLoad(_, _, _, index) => nat_map_set(found, index, True),
            WideStore(_, _, _, index, _) => nat_map_set(found, index, True),
            _ => found,
        }),
    }
}

fn StructuredRegion::indexes(self region: StructuredRegion, found: NatMap<Bool>) → NatMap<Bool> {
    match region {
        Dataflow(body) => nodes_indexes(body.nodes, found),
        Sequence(prefix, continuation) => continuation.indexes(nodes_indexes(prefix.nodes, found)),
        Compose(prefix, continuation) => continuation.indexes(prefix.indexes(found)),
        Conditional(_, _, _, _, when_true, when_false) => when_false.indexes(when_true.indexes(found)),
        Call(_, _, _, _) => found,
        Loop(_, _, _, _, body, _, _, _, _) => body.indexes(found),
        Lend(_, _, _, _) => found,
    }
}

fn nodes_ends(nodes: List<Node>, found: NatMap<Nat>) → NatMap<Nat> {
    match nodes {
        Nil => found,
        Cons(node, rest) => nodes_ends(rest, match node {
            WideLoad(bytes, _, _, index) => nat_map_set(found, index, bytes - 1),
            WideStore(bytes, _, _, index, _) => nat_map_set(found, index, bytes - 1),
            _ => found,
        }),
    }
}

fn StructuredRegion::ends(self region: StructuredRegion, found: NatMap<Nat>) → NatMap<Nat> {
    match region {
        Dataflow(body) => nodes_ends(body.nodes, found),
        Sequence(prefix, continuation) => continuation.ends(nodes_ends(prefix.nodes, found)),
        Compose(prefix, continuation) => continuation.ends(prefix.ends(found)),
        Conditional(_, _, _, _, when_true, when_false) => when_false.ends(when_true.ends(found)),
        Call(_, _, _, _) => found,
        Loop(_, _, _, _, body, _, _, _, _) => body.ends(found),
        Lend(_, _, _, _) => found,
    }
}

// What a function's search starts from: the words its loads and stores
// index by, how far each of several bytes reaches, and its parameters as
// themselves.
fn reads_counted(ids: List<Nat>, counts: NatMap<Nat>) → NatMap<Nat> {
    match ids {
        Nil => counts,
        Cons(id, rest) => reads_counted(rest, nat_map_set(counts, id, match nat_map_get(counts, id) { None => 1, Some(n) => n + 1 })),
    }
}

fn nodes_reads(nodes: List<Node>, counts: NatMap<Nat>) → NatMap<Nat> {
    match nodes { Nil => counts, Cons(node, rest) => nodes_reads(rest, reads_counted(node.reads(), counts)) }
}

fn ports_ids(ports: Ports) → List<Nat> { ports.entries.map(λ (port: Port) => port.id) }

// How many times each word is read: by a node, and once for each place a
// region names it (a port, a test, a loop's lists, a call's arguments).
fn StructuredRegion::reads(self region: StructuredRegion, counts: NatMap<Nat>) → NatMap<Nat> {
    match region {
        Dataflow(body) => nodes_reads(body.nodes, reads_counted(ports_ids(body.inputs) ++ ports_ids(body.outputs), counts)),
        Sequence(prefix, continuation) => continuation.reads(nodes_reads(prefix.nodes, reads_counted(ports_ids(prefix.inputs) ++ ports_ids(prefix.outputs), counts))),
        Compose(prefix, continuation) => continuation.reads(prefix.reads(counts)),
        Conditional(inputs, condition, arguments, outputs, when_true, when_false) =>
            when_false.reads(when_true.reads(reads_counted(Cons(condition, ports_ids(inputs) ++ (ports_ids(arguments) ++ ports_ids(outputs))), counts))),
        Call(_, arguments, _, _) => reads_counted(arguments, counts),
        Loop(inputs, bound, parameters, count, body, exit, next, outputs, exhausted) =>
            body.reads(reads_counted(Cons(bound, Cons(count, Cons(exit, Cons(exhausted, inputs ++ (parameters ++ (next ++ outputs)))))), counts)),
        Lend(views, _, arguments, _) => reads_counted(nodes_mentions(views_nodes(views)) ++ (views_homes(views) ++ arguments), counts),
    }
}

// Where the walk searches it has, of the whole function, how many times
// each word is read: a spelling that reads further forward pays only
// where it leaves the word it no longer reads unread (`Seen::frees`).
fn Function::seen(self function: Function, tables: Tables) → Seen {
    Seen::start(function.body.indexes(NatMap::empty()), function.body.ends(NatMap::empty()), match tables.asking {
        Zero => tables,
        Succ(_) => tables.reading(function.body.reads(reads_counted(Cons(function.result, function.also), NatMap::empty()))),
    }).of_parameters(function.parameters)
}

// The rules the search matches are the library's and those registered
// where the search is asked for, and the learners it asks those
// registered there: `entries` and `learners` are read where the call is.
// With `spellings` the walk searches at each node through that many of a
// word's other spellings (`Seen::searched`), and with `asking` too it
// reads the nodes rules wrote others for and regroups where that frees a
// word (`Seen::lends`): the walk after `find_helped`.
fn Function::learn_plan(self function: Function, entries: List<RuleEntry> := registered(RuleEntry), learners: List<Learner> := registered(Learner),
    spellings: Nat := 0, asking: Nat := 0) → Plan {
    shared(function.body.learn(function.seen(Tables::of(entries, learners).searching(spellings).asking_for(asking))), λ learned => learned.plan)
}

// The conditionals a plan replaces by an arm.
fn Plan::decided(self plan: Plan) → Nat {
    match plan {
        Same => 0,
        Nodes(_) => 0,
        Then(_, rest) => rest.decided(),
        Both(first, second) => first.decided() + second.decided(),
        Arms(when_true, when_false) => when_true.decided() + when_false.decided(),
        Arm(_, arm) => arm.decided() + 1,
        Body(body) => body.decided(),
        Derived(_, body) => body.decided(),
        Args(_) => 0,
        Inputs(_, body) => body.decided(),
        Flat(_, _) => 0,
    }
}

// The nodes a plan drops or writes.
fn steps_written(steps: List<Step>) → Nat {
    match steps {
        Nil => 0,
        Cons(step, rest) => match step { Drop => steps_written(rest) + 1, Emit(_) => steps_written(rest) + 1, _ => steps_written(rest) },
    }
}

fn Plan::written(self plan: Plan) → Nat {
    match plan {
        Same => 0,
        Nodes(steps) => steps_written(steps),
        Then(steps, rest) => steps_written(steps) + rest.written(),
        Both(first, second) => first.written() + second.written(),
        Arms(when_true, when_false) => when_true.written() + when_false.written(),
        Arm(_, arm) => arm.written(),
        Body(body) => body.written(),
        Derived(steps, body) => steps_written(steps) + body.written(),
        Args(_) => 0,
        Inputs(_, body) => body.written(),
        Flat(steps, _) => steps_written(steps),
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What to test at a function's entry

// The nodes of a test: a comparison of each parameter with a wanted bound
// with that bound, at ids from `next`, each or'd into the bit before it.
record Tests { nodes: List<Node>, bit: Option<Nat>, next: Nat }

fn named(names: NatMap<Nat>, id: Nat) → Nat { match nat_map_get(names, id) { None => id, Some(name) => name } }

// A node writing `output` and reading each id as `names` has it.
fn Node::renamed(self node: Node, names: NatMap<Nat>, output: Nat) → Node {
    match node {
        Const(w, _, v) => Const(w, output, v),
        Move(w, _, s) => Move(w, output, named(names, s)),
        Add(w, _, l, r) => Add(w, output, named(names, l), named(names, r)),
        Sub(w, _, l, r) => Sub(w, output, named(names, l), named(names, r)),
        BitAnd(w, _, l, r) => BitAnd(w, output, named(names, l), named(names, r)),
        BitOr(w, _, l, r) => BitOr(w, output, named(names, l), named(names, r)),
        BitXor(w, _, l, r) => BitXor(w, output, named(names, l), named(names, r)),
        ShiftLeft(w, _, v, n) => ShiftLeft(w, output, named(names, v), n),
        LogicalShiftRight(w, _, v, n) => LogicalShiftRight(w, output, named(names, v), n),
        RotateRight(w, _, v, n) => RotateRight(w, output, named(names, v), n),
        Apply(op, w, _, l, r) => Apply(op, w, output, named(names, l), named(names, r)),
        ArithmeticShiftRight(w, _, v, n) => ArithmeticShiftRight(w, output, named(names, v), n),
        UnsignedLessThan(w, _, l, r) => UnsignedLessThan(w, output, named(names, l), named(names, r)),
        SignedLessThan(w, _, l, r) => SignedLessThan(w, output, named(names, l), named(names, r)),
        Equal(w, _, l, r) => Equal(w, output, named(names, l), named(names, r)),
        BufferLoad(_, f, i) => BufferLoad(output, f, named(names, i)),
        BufferStore(_, f, i, v) => BufferStore(output, f, named(names, i), named(names, v)),
        Resize(from, to, _, s) => Resize(from, to, output, named(names, s)),
        SignResize(from, to, _, s) => SignResize(from, to, output, named(names, s)),
        TableRead(w, _, n, i, es) => TableRead(w, output, n, named(names, i), es),
        WideLoad(n, _, f, i) => WideLoad(n, output, f, named(names, i)),
        WideStore(n, _, f, i, v) => WideStore(n, output, f, named(names, i), named(names, v)),
        ByteSwap(w, _, s) => ByteSwap(w, output, named(names, s)),
    }
}

// A cone's nodes at ids from `next`, each reading the ones before it by
// their new ids.
record Renamed { nodes: List<Node>, names: NatMap<Nat>, next: Nat }

fn cone_renamed(cone: List<Node>, so_far: Renamed) → Renamed {
    match cone {
        Nil => so_far,
        Cons(node, rest) => cone_renamed(rest, Renamed(so_far.nodes ++ [node.renamed(so_far.names, so_far.next)],
            nat_map_set(so_far.names, node_output(node).id, so_far.next), so_far.next + 1)),
    }
}

// A span's test, where more than one comparison waits on it (a test that
// decides one is that one moved): its cone at new ids, the word `least`
// past its base, and two comparisons, each one where the test fails. The
// sum below the base is where the sum wrapped; the length below the sum is
// where the span does not fit. The body's own computation of the base is
// then a repeat of the cone's, which the pass that reads through copies
// writes as a move (`function_ir_tidy.hthr`).
fn span_tests(spans: List<Span>, so_far: Tests) → Tests {
    match spans {
        Nil => so_far,
        Cons(span, rest) => match span.uses.leb(1) {
            True => span_tests(rest, so_far),
            False => shared(cone_renamed(span.cone, Renamed([], NatMap::empty(), so_far.next)), λ cone =>
                shared(named(cone.names, span.base), λ base => shared(cone.next, λ k =>
                    shared(cone.nodes ++ [Const(64, k, span.least), Add(64, k + 1, base, k), UnsignedLessThan(64, k + 2, k + 1, base),
                        UnsignedLessThan(64, k + 3, span.length, k + 1)], λ nodes => span_tests(rest, match so_far.bit {
                        None => Tests(so_far.nodes ++ (nodes ++ [BitOr(1, k + 4, k + 2, k + 3)]), Some(k + 4), k + 5),
                        Some(bit) => Tests(so_far.nodes ++ (nodes ++ [BitOr(1, k + 4, bit, k + 2), BitOr(1, k + 5, k + 4, k + 3)]), Some(k + 5), k + 6),
                    }))))),
        },
    }
}

fn tests_of(parameters: List<Nat>, wants: NatMap<Nat>, so_far: Tests) → Tests {
    match parameters {
        Nil => so_far,
        Cons(parameter, rest) => match nat_map_get(wants, parameter) {
            None => tests_of(rest, wants, so_far),
            Some(least) => shared(so_far.next, λ k => tests_of(rest, wants, match so_far.bit {
                None => Tests(so_far.nodes ++ [Const(64, k, least), UnsignedLessThan(64, k + 1, parameter, k)], Some(k + 1), k + 2),
                Some(bit) => Tests(so_far.nodes ++ [Const(64, k, least), UnsignedLessThan(64, k + 1, parameter, k), BitOr(1, k + 2, bit, k + 1)],
                    Some(k + 2), k + 3),
            })),
        },
    }
}

// The test of every bound the function's undecided comparisons wait on, if
// there is one, at ids from `top`.
fn Function::guard_over(self function: Function, top: Nat, entries: List<RuleEntry> := registered(RuleEntry), learners: List<Learner> := registered(Learner)) → Option<Guard> {
    shared(function.body.learn(function.seen(Tables::of(entries, learners))), λ learned =>
        shared(span_tests(learned.seen.wants.spans, tests_of(function.parameters, learned.seen.wants.least, Tests([], None, top))), λ tests =>
            match tests.bit { None => None, Some(bit) => Some(Guard(top, tests.nodes, bit)) }))
}

fn Function::guard(self function: Function) → Option<Guard> { function.guard_over(function.top()) }
